<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Zengid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1127 CE – 1250 CE · capital Aleppo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Turkish dynasty of Zengi and Nur al-Din that united Muslim Syria against the Crusaders and prepared the way for Saladin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a>, son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk</a> governor, was appointed atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1127 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1128. In 1144 he captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-edessa">Edessa</a>, destroying the County of Edessa's capital, before being murdered in 1146.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1154 and united <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> revival with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, the Nuri hospital in Damascus, and a court for complaints, and presented his wars as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. He sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where Saladin became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> in 1169. Other Zengids ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Jazirat ibn Umar</a> as Turkish atabegs of a largely Arab and Kurdish population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> died in 1174, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and reduced the Zengid branches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> line lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a> displaced it in the 1230s, and minor branches survived until about 1250.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zengid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zengid dynasty" width="320" height="238"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zengid_territory.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1127 CE – 1250 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aleppo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Imad al-Din Zengi, Nur al-Din, Izz al-Din Mas'ud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Arabic, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zengids, Zengid Emirate, Zangids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=zengid-dynasty" aria-label="Zengid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5616 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zengid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5936 1127L5811 1337M5936 1127L5925 1159M5936 1127L5927 1218M5936 1127L6481 1127M5936 1127L5971 1095"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5925 1159h0M5927 1218h0M6481 1127h0M5971 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5936 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aleppo. <a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=zengid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
